## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Lithium-ion batteries have been a huge commercial success when it comes to powering portable electronic gadgets [1]. Lithium-ion batteries are also called ‘‘rocking chair batteries”, which use lithium ions to continuously undergo intercalation/ deintercalation and intercalation/deintercalation reactions between the cathode and anode electrodes to achieve the purpose of charging and discharging [2,3]. The anode electrode material of traditional lithium-ion batteries is graphite, and its theoretical capacity is only $372\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which is also a key factor restricting the high capacity of lithium-ion batteries [4]. When graphite is used as a anode electrode material, the reaction mechanism is an intercalation type reaction, which has the characteristics of low lithium intercalation potential, flat discharge potential, good conductivity, layered structure, and good crystal grains. The same layer of carbon atoms has a hexagonal network structure, and the layers are joined by the Van der Waals force, which allows lithium ions to be inserted and extracted [5]. The lithium insertion potential is between 0 and $0.2{\:}\mathsf{V}.$ Lithium-ion batteries with a low lithium insertion potential can produce greater voltage output. The anode electrode material of lithium-ion batteries, graphite, has a nice structure and strong cycle charge and discharge performance, making it one of the best alternatives. However, it also has disadvantages. In the process of deintercalation lithium from graphite materials, small solvent molecules in the electrolyte will be embedded in the graphite sheet together with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions. The solvent enters the graphite flakes and destroys the graphite flake structure, causing the graphite flakes to gradually peel off, losing the ability of lithium ions to intercalate lithium, reducing the reversible specific capacity of graphite, and shortening the cycle life. In addition, during the electrochemical reaction of the graphite carbon material, the graphite flakes are peeled off, which will cause the SEI film on the graphite surface to be damaged, and then a new SEI film will grow, which will cause the SEI film to become thicker and thicker. The ion diffusion path becomes longer and the rate performance decreases [6]. Finally, the electrochemical product of the reaction is LiC6. If the battery material generates gas and bulges, the electrochemically active $\mathrm{LiC}_{6}$ is prone to explosion hazards. In general, graphite-based anode materials need to be improved by fluorination or oxidation, coating, doping, surface treatment, and mechanical grinding. Despite its limited theoretical capacity, it can essentially fulfill the energy demands of some modern gadgets. As a result, developing new high specific energy electrode materials to improve their energy density and cycle life, or developing new energy storage battery systems based on high-storage elements, is a viable option for replacing traditional lithium-ion batteries and achieving industrial-grade applications [7].
Metal-organic frameworks materials and their derivatives, carbon materials, and metal compounds with unique nanostructures prepared by the metal–organic framework material template method have gradually become the ‘‘new force” of lithium-ion battery electrode materials [8,9]. MOFs materials have a series of inherent advantages such as high specific surface, high porosity, highly controllable composition, and structure, and have good application prospects in gas adsorption and separation, catalysis, fluorescent sensing, etc. [10–15]. Through simple heat treatment or solution reaction, metal–organic framework materials can be transformed into multifunctional active materials. The obtained metal–organic framework derivative material well inherits the original advantages of high porosity and high surface area and shows better electrochemical activity and stability than other new materials. In the realm of electrochemical energy storage and conversion, it is predicted to become a rising star [16–18]. The cathode materials of lithium-ion batteries are generally lithium-rich oxides with higher oxidation–reduction potentials, such as lithium cobalt oxide and lithium iron phosphate [19], which are difficult to obtain directly from metal-organic framework materials. The anode material is generally a porous carbon or metal compound with low redox reaction potential [20]. To solve the shortcomings of graphene, its electrochemical performance and structure are optimized by methods such as oxidation modification [21,22], coating modification [23,24], and etching modification [25,26]. However, transition metal compounds also have some shortcomings, such as poor conductivity, large changes in the volume of lithium ions during the deintercalation process, etc., which limit their application in lithium-ion batteries. Methods such as nanomaterials and composites with carbon-based materials can effectively solve the above shortcomings. Metal-organic framework materials are not only an emerging nanomaterial with a peculiar morphology but also an ideal precursor material for the preparation of metal oxides and carbon materials [27]. Especially by selecting metal–organic frameworks materials with high nitrogen content, high nitrogen content in the final composite carbon material can be achieved. At the same time, the porous structure can alleviate volume expansion, and the high carbon content in its skeleton can also improve the conductivity of the material, thus exhibiting excellent electrochemical performance. Some researchers have also begun to use heteroatoms (nitrogen, phosphorus, and sulfur) doping to improve the lithium storage performance of carbon materials [28,29]. By selecting organic ligands containing heteroatoms to construct metal–organic framework materials, carbon materials with highly dispersed heteroatoms can be obtained after pyrolysis. High nitrogen-doped graphene-like nanoparticles were prepared by direct carbonization of nitrogen-containing metal–organic framework material (ZIF-8), and the nitrogen content in the obtained carbon material was as high as $17.72\ \%$ [30]. After 50 cycles of circulation at a current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the material may retain a reversible capacity of $2132\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . It has a reversible capacity of $785\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles at a current density of $5\ \mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . In addition to the nanopores of graphene-like particles that can become active sites for lithium storage, doped nitrogen heteroatoms (especially pyridine nitrogen and pyrrole nitrogen) also provide abundant active sites and enhance the charge transport capability of the material [31]. When used as a sulfur carrier, the micron-scale porous structure can avoid the loss of active materials, reduce the shuttle effect, and ensure the electrochemical stability of the material [32,33].
Compared to previous work [34–36], this review focuses on the application and optimization strategies of MOFs for Li-ion battery electrodes. By summarizing the problems of current lithium-ion electrode materials for commercial applications, the performance of MOFs is targeted for optimization. We also discuss the practical application of these high-performance materials. This review begins with the goal of improving the structural stability and dynamic performance of lithium-ion battery electrode materials, and describes electrode material synthesis strategies based on MOFs and their derivatives, as illustrated in Fig. 1. By using MOFs to construct heterojunctions and network skeleton structures, the expansion and optimization of functional active sites are realized. Utilize the synergistic effect between composite materials to improve the electron and ion transmission dynamics, and obtain high-performance lithium-ion battery electrode materials. Based on the failure mechanism and optimization strategy of lithiumion battery electrode materials, MOFs are applied to the modification of lithium-ion electrode materials through the synthesis technology of junction MOFs. Finally, the future research directions of MOFs and Li-ion material design are prospected. The modified effect of MOFs on Li-ion battery materials is confirmed, and on the other hand, the performance of Li-ion batteries can be further extended by combining the two.
