## 2. The reaction mechanism and electrode optimization strategy of lithium ion battery
## 2.1. Lithium ion battery reaction mechanism
The charging and discharging mechanism of lithium-ion batteries are based on the ‘‘rocking chair battery” [37], which uses the $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ concentration difference to form a reversible deintercalation between the positive and anode materials to achieve charging and discharging. Taking a traditional $\mathsf{L i C o O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ battery as an example, as shown in Fig. 2, during the charging process, driven by an external electric field, ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ is extracted from the positive electrode material, enters the electrolyte, passes through the separator, and reaches the anode electrode and is embedded in the electrode material. The external circuit flows to the anode electrode and combines with $\operatorname{Li}+$ to cause an oxidation–reduction reaction. During the discharge process, $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ is released from the anode material and re-embedded in the cathode material to achieve a reversible cycle process. The charge and discharge reaction equation is as follows:
Cathode reaction: $L i C o O_{2}\leftrightarrow L i_{1-x}C o O_{2}+x e^{-}$ Anode reaction: $6C+x L i^{+}+x e^{-}\leftrightarrow L i_{x}C_{6}$ Total reaction: $L i C o O_{2}+6C\leftrightarrow L i_{1-x}C o O_{2}+L i_{x}C_{6}$
Compared with traditional nickel–cadmium/lead-acid batteries, lithium-ion batteries have the following advantages [39–42]: (1) Lithium metal has the lowest electrode potential $(-3.04\mathrm{~V})$ and lowest density $\mathrm{(M=6.94g{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ , $\uprho=0.53\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}.$ ), which can achieve high specific energy; (2) The working voltage is much higher than that of nickel–cadmium/lead-acid batteries, $3.6-$ $3.8~\mathrm{V}$ ; (3) The self-discharge efficiency is low and can be stored for a long time; (4) There is no memory effect, ensuring charging and discharging at any time; (5) Long cycle life, graphite replaces the lithium anode electrode, reduces the battery short circuit problem caused by lithium dendrites, and improves safety.
## 2.2. Optimal strategies for lithium-ion battery electrodes
## 2.2.1. Optimization of cathode materials
Lithium cobalt oxide is the earliest commercialized layered oxide cathode material. It belongs to the hexagonal crystal system. The $R{-}3~\mathrm{m}$ and $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{a}\mathsf{N a F e O}_{2}$ space group have a rock salt structure: ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ occupies 3a, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ occupies 3b, and $0^{2-}$ occupies 6c position. ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ are located in the octahedral void composed of O atoms, the transition metal layer and the lithium-ion layer are alternately arranged on the c-axis, and ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ can migrate between the layers. The advantages of lithium cobalt oxide are high voltage platform, stable electrochemical performance, and easy production. The disadvantages are limited reserves of precious metals, high material costs, and high toxicity. Although the theoretical gram capacity of lithium cobalt oxide is relatively high $(274\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}).$ , it can only release about $50\%$ of the capacity in practical applications. The reason is that when the degree of lithium cobalt oxide delithiation exceeds $50\%$ , its structure will rapidly develop Irreversible collapse. The production process of lithium cobalt oxide is relatively mature, and the common ones are the sol–gel method, coprecipitation method, and high-temperature solid-phase method. In recent years, research on lithium cobalt oxide has mainly focused on the improvement of material performance, specifically using surface coating, bulk doping, and other means to increase the structural stability of the material and improve ion mobility. Commonly used doping elements include lanthanum, nickel, iron, zirconium, and magnesium; common coating agents include carbon, oxides, etc. [46–48].
(1) Lithium iron phosphate $\left(\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}\right)$ ).
Lithium iron phosphate is an earlier developed cathode material. The space group of its olivine structure is Pnmb. O is located on the hexagonal close-packed surface and forms Fe-O octahedrons and $\mathtt{P-O}$ tetrahedra. Fe and Li occupy positions 4a and 4c, respectively. Because the tetrahedrons and octahedrons are arranged alternately on the same side, $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ forms a one-dimensional migration channel on the linear chain of alternating octahedrons. The advantages of lithium iron phosphate are abundant raw materials, good safety performance, good cycle performance, and lower cost. The disadvantage is that it is limited by the lithium-ion migration channel, resulting in poor conductivity and low-temperature performance, difficulty to improve power and rate performance, and inability to charge and discharge large currents. In view of the above defects, common modification methods include element doping and surface modification. Doping with metal elements (such as manganese, bismuth, zinc, zirconium, etc.) improves the diffusion rate of lithium ions in the bulk crystal lattice, thereby increasing the conductivity of the material. Improving interface properties by coating carbon materials can also improve electrical conductivity and low-temperature performance [49–51].
(2) Lithium manganese oxide $\left(\mathrm{LiMn_{2}O_{4}}/\mathrm{LiMnO_{2}}\right)$ .
Lithium manganese oxide can be divided into spinel-type $\left(\mathrm{LiMn}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}\right)$ and layered type $\left(\mathrm{LiMnO}_{2}\right).$ ) according to its structure. Spinel-type lithium manganate is widely used because it can provide three-dimensional lithium-ion diffusion channels. It belongs to the cubic crystal system, $F d{-}3~\mathrm{~m~}$ space group, O atoms are stacked as face-centered cubic, occupying the 32e position, $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ occupies the 8a position, and Mn occupies the 16d position. The advantages of spinel lithium manganate material are good safety performance, low price, good rate, and power performance [52]. The disadvantages are that the gram capacity is too low, the Jahn-Teller effect in the charge and discharge process causes structural distortion, manganese is easily dissolved, and oxygen defects in the structure. These problems severely limit the promotion and application of lithium manganate materials. The method to improve the above defects is still coating and doping [53,54].
(3) Ternary materials $\mathrm{(LiNi_{x}C o_{x}M_{1-x-y}O_{2})}$ .
Ternary materials are the general name of $\mathrm{LiNi_{x}C o_{x}M_{1-x-y}O_{2}}$ , which can be divided into many types according to the ratio of nickel, cobalt, and manganese. Common ternary materials are $\mathrm{LiNi}_{0.3}\mathrm{Co}_{0.3}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.3}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (NCM111), $\mathrm{LiNi}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.2}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.3}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (NCM523), $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.6}C o_{0.2}M n_{0.2}O_{2}}$ (NCM622), and $\mathrm{LiNi}_{0.8}\mathrm{Co}_{0.1}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (NCM811). Among them, nickel content of $60\%$ or more is called a highnickel ternary material [55,56]. The ternary material is similar in structure to lithium cobalt oxide, both belong to the cubic crystal system, and the space group is $\scriptstyle{R-3\mathrm{~m~}}$ , $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{a}\mathsf{I}\mathsf{a}\mathsf{F e}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ . Due to the close physical and chemical properties of nickel, cobalt, and manganese, it is easy to form a solid solution, that is, part of the cobalt in the $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ structure is replaced by nickel and manganese, but the rock salt structure is still maintained [57,58]. The ratios of nickel, cobalt, and manganese are different, and the electrochemical properties of the materials are also different. Among ternary materials, cobalt exhibits $\mathbf{+}\mathbf{\beta}_{3}$ valence, while nickel and manganese have $^{+\ 2}$ and $+4$ valences. During charge and discharge, the valence of the manganese element remains unchanged, which contributes to the stable structure of the material. The battery capacity comes from all $^+2$ valent nickel and some $^+3$ cobalt, so the higher the nickel content, the higher the gram capacity of the material [59,60]. The contribution of cobalt is to improve electrochemical performance, such as rate, power, low-temperature performance, etc. [61]. Since the valence state of Ni in high nickel ternary materials is still dominated by $^+3$ valence, there is structural instability. Nickel dissolution (derived from the disproportionation reaction) and structural distortion (derived from the Jahn-Teller effect) will occur during the cycle, which will transform the ternary layer structure to the rock salt structure. The main manifestation is poor cycle performance, especially under high-temperature conditions [62]. Ion doping is often used to solve the problems of hightemperature structure instability, cycle, and poor safety performance of high nickel ternary materials. Doping elements generally include Mg, Cr, Al, Na, Zr, etc. [63–65]. Studies have shown that ion doping can make the overall structure of the material more stable and reduce the degree of cation mixing, thereby effectively improving the cycle performance of the battery [66,67]. The high nickel ternary material has high oxidability when the charge is cut off, which will cause the electrolyte to oxidize and form a film on the surface of the positive electrode, which will cause the internal resistance to increase. In addition, due to the high-pressure oxidation of the electrolyte itself, the cycle performance will deteriorate [68,69]. To solve this problem, the common improvement method is surface coating modification. The coating materials include ${\sf A l}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Li}3\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and C [70–72]. Studies have shown that the coating technology can ‘‘separate” the cathode material and the electrolyte, effectively reducing the side reactions at the interface, thereby improving the stability of the material and the cycle performance of the battery [73,74].
## 2.2.2. Optimization of anode materials
As one of the key materials of lithium-ion batteries, the anode material directly affects the performance and safety of the battery [75,76]. Common types of anode electrodes are:
(1) Graphite.
Graphite is the earliest commercialized anode electrode material and has many advantages: a) Low lithium insertion potential $({\sim}0.1~\mathsf{V})$ , close to lithium metal potential (0 V), and lower than the decomposition potential of most electrolytes, thus making Lithium-ion batteries have a higher working voltage; b) The theoretical specific capacity is high $({\sim}372\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ), and the current mature commercial materials can reach a specific capacity of more than $365\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which is close to the theoretical specific capacity; c) The raw materials are abundant and the price is low. At present, the price of commercial graphite has stabilized (within 4,722 US dollars/ton), which is conducive to the cost reduction of batteries [77,78].
The disadvantages of graphite materials are also obvious: a) The specific surface area is large, and it is easy to form a thick SEI film in contact with the electrolyte, resulting in low efficiency at the first time [79]; b) The layered structure of graphite is easy to collapse during the battery cycle. It is manifested by the decrease of battery capacity and cycle diving [80]; c) In the process of highrate charging, due to the limited lithium insertion rate of the layered structure, some ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ cannot be embedded in the graphite layer in time and precipitated to form lithium dendrites. With the growth of lithium dendrites, safety issues can arise [81–83].
To improve graphite material defects, common methods are: a) Surface coating. By coating a layer of amorphous carbon or metal oxide on the graphite surface, the compatibility of graphite and electrolyte can be significantly improved, and the rate charge and discharge performance can be improved [84,85]. Meanwhile, the coating will also reduce the specific surface area of the material and reduce the formation of SEI film, thereby reducing the loss of irreversible lithium and improving the first efficiency [86,87]. b) Sphericalization. The scale structure of the graphite shape is changed by mechanical pulverization, which reduces the contact area between the material and the electrolyte, thereby improving the first efficiency and reversible capacity of the battery [88,89].
(2) Silicon-based materials.
Silicon-based materials have a very high theoretical gram capacity of $4200\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which is much superior to graphite materials $372\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ). The number of anode electrodes utilized in the battery design process may be greatly decreased, but the energy density of the battery can be enhanced [90,91]. However, silicon-based materials will undergo significant volume expansion and structural collapse during charging and discharging, and the corresponding SEI (solid electrolyte interphase) film on the surface will also experience the process of ‘‘destruction-regeneration-des truction-regeneration. . .” repeatedly. The lithium source consumed by repairing the SEI film appears as an irreversible decline in battery capacity [92]. Such materials have low conductivity and cannot be charged and discharged with high power [93].
Anode materials are an important part of lithium-ion batteries and play a key role in achieving high energy density and longcycle stability and quality. However, there are still the following problems and challenges that need to be resolved: (a) Theoretical specific capacity of traditional graphite anodes Low, only 372 $\mathrm{\mAh/g}$ ; (b) The stress caused by lattice distortion in the process of releasing Li will cause the electrode material to smash and fall off and reduce the cycle stability; (c) Poor conductivity, transition metal oxides and silicon The intrinsic conductivity of electrode materials such as, germanium, etc. is low, which increases the internal resistance of the battery and reduces the electrochemical activity. Research has found that a series of methods such as nanometerization [94], morphology control [95], and carbon coating [96] can effectively enhance the performance of the material. However, traditional carbon coating modification methods have problems such as coating uniformity and coating amount. To increase the cycle stability and reaction kinetics of lithium-ion battery anode materials, the following characteristics are required [97,98]: (a) It has a low redox potential, ensures high output voltage, and achieves high energy density; (b) It has a stable structure that is conducive to rapid deintercalation and achieves long-term cycle stability; (c) It has high conductivity and good charge transport channels to ensure rapid coupling and transport of ions and electrons, improve reaction kinetics, and achieve high rate performance (See Table 2).
For lithium-ion battery anode materials, the main design and preparation strategies are summarized in the following three points [97,99,100]: (a) Constructing highly conductive matrix composite materials to improve structural stability and intrinsic conductivity; (b) Constructing nanometers porous electrodes increase active sites and relieve volume expansion; (c) Introduce electrode materials with low potential and high theoretical specific capacity to achieve high energy density. By controlling the derivation conditions, MOFs can be derivatized into porous carbon materials, porous metal-based materials, and porous carbon/metalbased composite materials, which have both porous characteristics and introduce high-capacity and low-potential active materials to obtain high-specific energy lithium-ion battery anode materials.
3. Preparation and application of MOFs compounds and their derivative structures in lithium-ion batteries
Based on the advantages of MOFs, such as porosity, controllable morphology, easily tunable components, and easy functionalization, MOFs are increasingly used as ideal precursors to prepare derivatives of various compositions and structures. Including heteroatom-doped carbon, metals, metal oxides and their complexes, etc., it has a good prospect in the field of energy storage and conversion, especially in the field of lithium-ion batteries [101]. MOFs-derived nanostructures have many unique advantages: (1) by designing and synthesizing various MOFs, their chemical compositions can be easily tuned and combined with specific heat treatment processes to prepare derivative materials with various functional structures; (2) MOFs-derived nanostructures The nanostructure provides controllable porosity and huge surface area, which greatly increases the electrode/electrolyte contact area, which can give full play to the performance of electrode materials; (3) greatly shortens the diffusion paths of electrons and ions, which is important for improving MOFs derivatization. The rate capability of nanostructured electrodes is very favorable; (4) low-cost, easy-to-synthesize MOFs-derived nanostructures offer potential large-scale production routes for eventual industrial applications [102,103].
The current research focuses and difficulties are: (1) Controllable synthesis of precursors. Design and synthesize MOFs precursors with novel structures by adjusting metal ions and ligands; (2) derivatives.
controllable transformation. Combining the characteristics of the precursor, adjusting and optimizing the transformation conditions, the structure and composition of the derivative are controllable, and advanced functional materials with specific functional structures and excellent components are prepared as electrode materials for high-performance lithium-ion batteries.
## 3.1. Mofs compounds and their derivative structures
The metal–organic framework material is a kind of organic– inorganic hybrid functional porous material composed of a metal central node and an organic linker and belongs to a kind of coordination polymer [104]. Among them, metal nodes generally refer to metal ions or metal clusters, and organic linkers are multidentate organic ligands containing oxygen, nitrogen, and sulfur. Metal ions and organic ligands self-assemble through coordination bonds to form metal–organic framework materials with various structures. A novel form of inorganic–organic hybrid functional porous material is the metal–organic framework material, which has the characteristics of stable inorganic materials, clear crystal structure, and modifiable organic materials. Combining the inorganic and organic parts into a unified whole makes the metal–organic framework material possess some properties that are not possessed by individual inorganic or organic materials.
The specific surface area of MOFs is generally above $2000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , far exceeding the traditional activated carbon and molecular sieve materials [105]. PCN (porous coordination network) series have larger holes [106], among which the BET specific surface area of PCN-61 $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}0)_{3}$ -(btei) $13\mathrm{H}_{2}0{\cdot}33\mathrm{dmf})$ , PCN-66 $(\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H}_{2}0)_{3}$ -(ntei) $13\mathrm{H}_{2}0{\cdot}33\mathrm{dmf})$ and PCN-68 $\mathrm{{'}C u}_{3}(\mathrm{{H}_{2}O)_{3}}$ -(ptei) $13\mathrm{H}_{2}0{\cdot}33\mathrm{dmf})$ can reach 3000, 4000, $5109~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, and the pore volume can reach 1.36, 1.63, $2.03~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. In addition, the BET specific surface area of NU-110 $\mathbf{\nabla}\mathrm{NU}~=~\mathbf{\nabla}$ Northwestern University; NU-100 is also known as PCN$610[106]_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ reported by Farha et al. can reach $7140~\mathrm{\m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . It is the highest known specific surface area of a solid material [107].
Compared with traditional porous materials, MOFs formed by self-assembly of host metal ions and guest organic ligands have a gentler synthesis method, showing the advantages of species, structure diversity, high specific surface area, high stability, and adjustable pore structure. Keeping the metal ions unchanged and introducing organic ligands with different carbon chain lengths, a series of MOFs with similar structures but different pore sizes can be obtained to achieve highly adjustable porosity, such as the IRMOFs (isoreticular metal–organic frameworks) series (Fig. 3a) [108]. On the contrary, keeping the organic ligands unchanged and regulating the metal ions, according to the coordination number and coordination mode of the metal ions, MOFs with similar or completely different structures can be obtained, such as the ZIF (zeolitic imidazolate framework) series with similar structures. Using organic ligands as a carrier and introducing specific functional groups or impurity atoms can obtain different chemical properties of MOFs and heteroatoms precisely doped derived carbon materials (Fig. 3b) [109]. In addition, the use of the porous and large specific surface characteristics of MOFs and their derivative structures as a matrix to immobilize active materials to achieve spatial confinement, and to use the multi-level synergistic effect between host and guest materials to achieve the design optimization of functional activation sites.
The characteristics of MOFs and their derivative structures are mainly summarized into the following three aspects:
(1) Diversity of structure and function. The rich diversity of metal ions and organic ligands brings the diversity of structure and function of MOFs derived materials. The differences in valence and coordination modes between different metal ions, the length of the carbon chain of organic ligands, and the modification of functional groups have greatly broadened the diversity of MOFs. According to different processing methods, different morphological structures, such as solid, hollow, and core–shell structures, and derivative materials of different components can be obtained. In addition, according to the required characteristics, the introduction of functional composite materials can further broaden the structure and function of derivative materials [112,113].
(2) High-efficiency interface charge transport capability. The rich diversity of pore structure can be achieved by adjusting the length of the ligand carbon chain and the template method. MOFs-derived materials inherit their porous characteristics and large specific surface area, which can increase the contact area of the electrode/electrolyte, optimize the distribution of active sites, and realize the efficient coupling and transport of electrons and ions, ensuring efficient interface charge transport capabilities [114,115].
(3) High-efficiency spatial confinement effect. Using a rich and adjustable pore structure, introduce guest materials, and accurately control the composition, content, and dispersion of MOFs and guest materials. In addition, the spatial confinement effect of the pore structure can ensure the uniform and efficient immobilization of the active material [116,117].
To achieve high-performance storage, the design and preparation principles of lithium-ion battery anodes are summarized as follows: (1) It has a certain mechanical strength, flexibility, and porosity, which can improve the dispersion of the active material in the matrix material. While ensuring a high utilization rate, it alleviates the failure of electrode powdering caused by volume expansion during the cycle, and achieves long-cycle stability; (2) It has high-efficiency interface charge transport capability, and realizes the rapid coupling and separation of electrons and ions. Improve the electrode reaction rate and achieve high rate performance; (3) The electrode material has enough Li $^+$ storage active sites to ensure high specific energy. Fig. 3(c) shows the diversity of MOFs derived structures [111]. Combined with electrode material design guidelines, MOFs and their derivative materials have great application prospects in the field of energy storage. Compared with traditional materials, MOFs have a highly ordered structure and adjustable metal ions and organic ligands [118], which can well control the structural diversity and flexibility of MOFs. The porosity of MOFs can form a larger space and a suitable channel, which facilitates the storage and transmission of lithium ions [119]. Through the component design and nanostructure of MOFs composite materials, active centers with high activity and stability can be prepared, so as to obtain efficient and durable electrode materials [120]. However, the conductivity of MOFs is poor. To improve the conductivity of MOFs it is necessary to electrochemically modify MOFs to improve the electrochemical properties of MOFs [121]. MOF-based materials must somehow preserve the benefits of MOFs, but also overcome the drawbacks of MOFs’ poor conductivity, improving the chemical stability of lithium-ion battery applications.
3.2. Design and preparation strategies of MOFs compounds and their derivative structures on lithium-ion batteries
Fig. 3(d) summarizes the main strategies of MOFs-based material modification design [110]. Combining the structural characteristics of MOFs and the energy storage mechanism of secondary batteries, to improve the stability of the electrode structure and improve the mechanics of charge transport, the design and preparation strategies of MOFs and their derivative materials are classified into methods such as regulating metal sites, organic ligands, pore structure, and constructing heterostructures.
## 3.2.1. Adjust metal sites to achieve component optimization
By altering the types and proportions of metals, bimetal and even multimetal MOF precursors can be made using the similarity of metal sites. By controlling the derivatization conditions, the metal sites of MOFs can be selectively retained and agglomerated into metal particles/carbon composites during the hightemperature carbonization process, and the derivatization conditions can be further adjusted to obtain single or composite metal compounds, such as elemental metals [122], metal oxide [123], metal phosphide [124], metal sulfide [125] and metal single atom [126], etc. Using Co/Ni-MOFs as precursors [127], the carbonization temperature is adjusted to promote the agglomeration of dispersed metal sites into metal particles. Under the high catalytic action of Co/Ni, the carbonized organic ligands are cracked to derive carbon nanotubes (CNTs). This method gives full play to the synergistic effect of metal sites and organic ligands and has a certain versatility in the in-situ derivation of CNTs.
With the rich structure of MOFs as the frame, metal oxides with different morphologies can be designed, and metal ions can be converted into metal oxides through high-temperature carbonization. While inheriting the porous characteristics of MOFs, the uniform distribution of active materials can be achieved. Using bi-coneshaped MIL-88-Fe as the precursor [123], controlling the carbonization atmosphere, successfully transformed it into spindleshaped $\mathsf{a}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{F}\mathsf{e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ . HRTEM shows that the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ particle size is less than $20\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4a). The reduction in the particle size of the active material and the increase in the degree of dispersion will greatly increase the utilization rate of the active material. The porous structure can buffer internal stress, relieve volume expansion, and improve the structural stability of the electrode material. After 50 cycles at a current density of 0.2C, it can retain a specific capacity of $911\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ as an anode electrode material for lithium storage. It can retain a specific capacity of $424\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ even when the current density is as high as 10C, demonstrating exceptional rate performance. Further control the subsequent synthesis and transformation conditions, such as sulfurization [128], phosphating [129], nitridation [130], selenization [131], etc., which can convert MOFs-derived metal oxides into specific metal compounds and broaden the components of MOFs derived structure. The carbonized MOFs, including solid structure $\mathsf{C u@C}$ , hollow structure ${\mathsf{N i O@C}}$ and pod-like ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}0_{3}@C$ were mixed with Se powder [125], and ${\mathsf{C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S e@C}}$ , ${\mathsf{N i S e@C}}$ , and ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}S{\mathrm{e}}_{8}@C$ were obtained after hightemperature selenization. During the selenization process, the unique morphology and structure of MOFs are maintained well, which further confirms the structural stability of MOFs-derived materials. The synthesis diagram is shown in Fig. 4(b).
When different metal ions have the same coordination number with the same organic ligand, multiple metals can be introduced into the precursor to derive bimetal/ polymetallic compounds [132–134]. Using the interaction between several metals in MOFs, metal sites in MOF-derived materials can be enriched. Utilizing the infinite miscibility of the ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}/C\mathrm{o}$ in ZIF [135], the $Z\mathrm{n}/C\mathrm{o}$ coexisting ZIF precursor can be grown in situ on the foamed nickel to build a Ni/ZIF/RGO sandwich structure (Fig. 4c), and in-situ derived carbon-coated bimetallic oxide $Z n C o_{2}O_{4}{-}Z_{n}O$ . Compared with single metal oxides, there is a synergistic effect between double metal oxides. Due to the difference in oxidation–reduction potential, the stress caused by ion insertion and extraction can be dispersed, the structural stability of the electrode material can be improved, and long cycle life can be obtained. In-situ derived porous carbon can improve the conductivity of active materials, enhance the charge transport and electrochemical reaction kinetics, and achieve high rate performance.
Metal single-atom catalysts have unique quantum effects. Because of their high atom utilization, high catalytic activity, and high selectivity, they have received extensive attention in the field of catalysis. The unique topological structure and abundant N elements of MOFs can achieve a high degree of dispersion of metal sites and capture of elemental metals and are the most effective precursors for achieving high-load metal single-atom catalysts. As shown in Fig. $4(\mathrm{d})$ , using Co to replace part of the Zn atoms in ZIF-8 and dilute the Co atoms. During the subsequent carbonization process, the highly dispersed Zn atoms can effectively prevent the Co atoms from agglomerating into large particles, realizing a single atom of Co. The Zn element is fixed, and the Zn element is evaporated at about $900\mathrm{~\textdegree~}$ , to obtain the $\mathsf{C o N}_{4}/\mathsf C$ structure with high catalytic activity [136]. In addition, adjusting the ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}/C\mathrm{o}$ in ZIF can achieve precise control of Co from single atoms to nanoclusters to metal particles. Using the ZIF-8 structure, the coordination of transition metals such as $\mathrm{Fe/Ni/Cu}$ can also be realized. Therefore, this strategy is also applicable to the preparation of other transition metal single-atom catalysts [137,138].
Metal-MOFs, as a kind of MOF, may be employed directly as a lithium-ion battery electrode material due to their large specific surface area and abundance of pores. Take Mn-MOFs as an example, MnO has proved to be the most promising anode electrode material for lithium-ion batteries due to its theoretical high capacitance, low cost, and environmental friendliness, but it also has many shortcomings, such as poor cycle stability during charging and discharging [139,140]. Mn-MOFs-derived metal oxide materials have a larger specific surface area and unique porous structure than ordinary materials. This allows it to have more reaction sites, shorten the distance of ion transmission, and improve the conductivity of ions and electrons [141]. Furthermore, the carbonization of MOFs, which have a large specific surface area and strong electrical conductivity and are extremely competitive in the application of electrode materials, can yield carbon materials with diverse morphologies [142,143]. The current density is 0.1 and $1.0\ \mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ under the $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ potential window of $0.01{-}2.0\mathrm{~V~}$ when the solvothermal technique is employed to manufacture Mn-BTC [144], and it is directly utilized as an anode electrode material in lithium-ion batteries. The battery’s specific capacity ranges between 694 and $400\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5a). The research results show that Mn-BTC as anode electrode material has good cycling ability, capacity retention ability, and ability to maintain MOF network. To synthesize a manganese-based layered coordination polymer with a microporous structure [145], H2tfbdc and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy and manganese acetate tetrahydrate (II) were used to prepare Mn(tfbdc) $(4,4\ ^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy)(H_{2}O)_{2}(M n\mathrm{-LCP)}}$ (Fig. 5b). Through the characterization of the material structure, it can be found that there is a weak antiferromagnetic exchange of $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ ions in Mn-LCP. MnLCP displays an irreversible high capacitance during the initial discharge process when employed as an anode electrode material for lithium-ion batteries. However, from the fourth cycle onwards, the reversible specific capacity can reach $390{\mathrm{~mAh.g}}^{-1}$ . Using 1,4-BDC (1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) and $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}$ as raw materials [146], the MOF material synthesized by solvothermal method-Mn-1,4- BDC (Mn-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid), after treatment at a high temperature of $200\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}$ to remove its coordination solvent, Mn1,4-BDC $@200$ can be obtained. Respectively use it as anode electrode material in a lithium-ion battery (Fig. 5c). After 100 cycles at a current density of $100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the Mn-1,4-BDC $@200$ electrode material can still retain the reversible specific capacity of 974 $\mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Compared with Mn-1,4-BDC, it shows better electrochemical performance. It has been found that the coordination solvent pair in the MOF structure of Mn-benzenedicarboxylic acid (Mn-BDC) has adverse effects on electrochemical performance. In addition, the excellent electrochemical performance of Mn-1,4- BDC directly as an anode electrode material is comparable to the reported Mn-MOF-derived manganese oxide electrode material. When used single-crystal Mn-PBA synthesized by coprecipitation at room temperature (Fig. 5d), namely $\mathsf{M n}[F e$ $(C N)_{6}]_{0.6667\cdot\mathrm{nH}_{2}O}$ as anode electrode material for lithium-ion batteries, Mn- PBA has the advantages of large capacitance, good rate performance, good cycle stability, and high coulomb efficiency [147]. After being cycled 100 times at a current density of $200\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , a specific capacity of $295.7\ \mathrm{\mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ can still be obtained. This may be due to its inherent characteristics, such as the three-dimensional polymer frame can promote the transportation of lithium ions during the charging and discharging process of the electrode. On the other hand, Mn-PBA has a wider free space that can contain not only transition metal ions and tiny molecules, but also redox processes such as changing the iron valence state to establish charge balance and improve electrochemical performance. The MOFs structure can also be used to realize different reaction mechanisms of lithium-ion batteries, such as the ‘‘bipolar charging” mechanism (Fig. 5e) [148]. In a single redox cycle, not only lithium ions but also larger anions can be individually inserted into the pores of the MOF, thereby increasing the total capacitance of the material. Using this ‘‘bipolar charging” mechanism, a universal design method can be proposed. In addition, the MOFs material using this mechanism has significant reactivity and cycle stability and can become a universal electrode material for a variety of batteries. But this mechanism is currently only applicable to porous materials because it needs space inside to accommodate large anions.
## 3.2.2. Regulate organic ligands to achieve precise doping of heteroatoms
Porous carbon materials have both high electrical conductivity and high physical, chemical, and structural stability, so they have great application potential in the field of energy storage. Design and prepare low-cost and high-performance porous carbon materials, and regulate their specific surface area and pore size distribution. The use of heteroatom doping to functionally modify carbon materials can induce charge migration and rearrangement, which can improve intrinsic conductivity and strengthen inherent characteristics. Taking N atom doping as an example, the similar radius makes it easier to replace C atoms, and N atoms have one more extranuclear electron than C atoms. After doping, the charge density of adjacent C atoms will be increased, thereby increasing the intrinsic conductivity of carbon materials. MOFs are rich in organic ligands and can be used as self-sacrificial templates to prepare porous carbon materials. The introduction of specific functional groups through organic ligands, such as elements containing $\mathsf{N}/\mathsf{P}/$ S/B, can achieve precise control of the position and content of heteroatoms. It is then derived into a porous carbon material doped with in-situ heteroatoms to realize the functionalization of active sites [30,149–152].
The ZIF series with dimethylimidazole as the organic ligand is rich in N elements. Using it as a self-sacrificial template for hightemperature carbonization can derive in-situ $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous carbon materials, further control the carbonization temperature, and achieve precise control of the N content. Using ZIF-8 as the precursor [30], a porous carbon material with an N content of up to $17.72\mathrm{wt\%}$ was obtained by pyrolysis (Fig. 6a). The specific capacity of this electrode material is still as high as $2132\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles at a current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which is substantially greater than the theoretical capacity of graphite-like carbon materials. Theoretical calculations show that the pyrrole N and pyridine N on the edge of the graphite can adsorb more Li $^+$ as active sites and contribute to an ultra-high specific capacity. Adjust the position of organic ligands that modify different functional groups, and use the difference in charge density between heteroatoms to bring heteroatoms to the interface, form a potential difference, build a built-in electric field, and improve the mechanics of charge transport. Using UiO-66 as the matrix [149], organic ligands can be functionalized. Use it as the core for secondary growth of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO66 to construct a $\mathsf{U i O{-}66@U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ core–shell structure. The chemical reaction of melamine phosphate and $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ is used to successfully introduce P-containing organics, and after further carbonization treatment, N is obtained, P gradient heterogeneous doped C@NAC@N, PAC composite material (Fig. 6b). As shown in Fig. 6(c), the gradient doping of heterogeneous atoms triggers the rearrangement of charges, and a potential difference is formed inside the particles to obtain a gradient band structure. This greatly enhances the intrinsic conductivity of the derived carbon materials, accelerates charge transport, and improves reaction kinetics.
The specific capacity of lithium battery cathode materials $\langle\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ , LiFePO4) in the market is not optimistic, and the rate performance is poor. Other materials, such as $\mathsf{L i M n}_{\mathrm{x}}0_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathrm{LiNiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{LiNi_{x}}$ ${\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{Mn}}_{\mathrm{x}}{0}_{2}$ , etc., are also used in cathode materials, but the capacity is difficult to exceed $300\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The unique pore structure of MOFs can not only provide a larger space for lithium storage, but also provide a guarantee for the mobile transport of lithium ions. In recent years, more and more researches have focused on the preparation of MOFs-derived cathode materials with high energy density and high-power density.
Liu et al. [153] directly prepared nitrogen-doped LiFeO4 and carbon porous composites through MIL-100 (Fe) naturally occurring iron and carbon sources by means of derivative preparation. The charge–discharge capacity of $\mathrm{LiFeO}_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{N}}$ at 20C is 93.6 $\mathrm{m}{\tt A h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . This is due to the porous structure and the doping of heteroatoms in the composite, which provide more lithium storage sites for the electrode, so the specific capacity increases. At the same time, the composite of carbon materials greatly improves the electrical conductivity of the composite materials. Liao et al. [154] prepared carbon-coated $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}$ materials by coreaction of MIL-101, $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ and LiOH for the first time through a high-temperature preparation method. Excellent. Huo et al. [155] first prepared V-MOFs precursors with different morphologies, and then prepared P-containing $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}@C$ composite materials with special structure by composite and high-temperature carbonization, which can be used in small currents. It has a capacity of $138\mathrm{\mAh\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a density of 0.1C, a specific capacity of 65 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 10C, and can reach $90\%$ of the initial capacity after 1000 cycles.
## 3.2.3. Adjust the pores to build a hierarchical porous structure
$\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ will produce lattice distortion during the process of inserting and extracting from the electrode material, triggering internal stress, causing the structure of the electrode material to deform, collapse, smash, and even fall off from the current collector, causing irreversible degradation of battery capacity. The nanoporous structure can expose more active sites, increase the electrode/electrolyte contact area, shorten the ion transport path, and improve the charge transport dynamics and electrochemical activity. This provides a buffer space for the internal stress and volume expansion generated in the process of deintercalation lithium, ensuring structural stability and achieving high cycle stability. Therefore, the controllable construction of porous electrode materials with structural stability and mechanical integrity, and reducing the capacity attenuation caused by deformation and stress, is the key to the development of high-performance electrode materials. Porous electrode materials have the following characteristics: (1) Buffer volume expansion, improve structural stability; (2) Increase charge/electrolyte contact area, increase electrochemically active sites; (3) Easy charge transfer and diffusion, improve transport mechanics. The construction of a hierarchical porous structure to meet different functional requirements will greatly increase the utilization rate of active materials and improve electrochemical performance. The precursor of topological MOFs has a rich pore structure, and its derivative materials usually inherit porous characteristics, and the pore structure is highly ordered and adjustable. It has great application prospects as an electrode material [156– 159].
Studies have found that with MOF-5 as the precursor [157], after high-temperature calcination at $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the specific surface area of the derived porous carbon material is as high as $2872\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The large specific surface area is mainly derived from two aspects, the porous structure of the MOFs themselves, and the vacancies generated by the evaporation of metal Zn during the high-temperature calcination process. Most of the MOFs precursors reported so far are mainly microporous, but the singlechannel structure has limitations in application. Extending the carbon chain of organic ligands can adjust the size of the pores, but too long a carbon chain can make the carbonized derivative structure unstable and easy to collapse. Therefore, based on constructing a hierarchical pore structure precursor with adjustable pores and coexistence of micro-mesopores, improving the stability of itself and its derivative structure is an effective means to broaden the application field of MOFs and their derivative materials. By using polystyrene spheres (PS) of different sizes as templates [158], a two-solvent-induced heterogeneous nucleation method is used to ensure that MOFs grow in situ in the voids of the PS template to form a single crystal ZIF-8 with an orderly and adjustable pore structure (Fig. 7a). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller specific surface area and micropore volume of SOM-ZIF-8(3, 0.5) were $1540~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.59\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively. This method is also applicable to the preparation of ZIF-67 with the same crystal structure. The single crystal ZIF-67 with coexisting ordered micro-mesopores can be used as the precursor [159] to derive three-dimensional ordered mesoporous ${\mathsf{C o S e}}_{2}@C$ (Fig. 7b). The Brunauer-EmmettTeller(BET) specific surface area and micropore volume of SOMZIF-67(3, 0.5) were $1467.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.57~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}.$ respectively. Micro-mesopores with their coexisting structure may considerably increase the structural stability of living materials, guarantee that the electrolyte is thoroughly penetrated, offer more effective active sites, strengthen charge transport dynamics, and enhance electrochemical performance.
Ge, as an anode electrode material for lithium-ion batteries, has a theoretical specific capacity of $1600\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and a conductivity of $47\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}$ , which is four orders of magnitude higher than Si, but it also faces structural damage caused by volume expansion. Using the PS sphere as the template [160], using the electrochemical deposition method, Ge is uniformly coated on the PS sphere, and after removing the template, a self-supporting three-dimensional ordered macroporous Ge electrode is obtained, as shown in Fig. 8 (a-b). The pore diameters are about $420~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ . The threedimensional ordered macroporous structure increases the electrode/electrolyte contact interface, exposing more active sites and forming fast charge transport channels. Furthermore, the porous structure may effectively mitigate the volume expansion caused by lithium deintercalation, minimize the internal stress on the Ge electrode, and increase the battery’s cycle stability. Cycling at a current density of $320\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 50 cycles can result in a specific capacity of $844\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Using the porous structure of
MOFs, a hierarchical porous core–shell structure $\mathsf{C@M o S_{2}}$ composite material [161] was prepared, and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ was uniformly loaded on the in-situ derived C spheres, as shown in Fig. 8(c-d). The diameter of the monodisperse microspheres is uniformly $500~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and the nanosheets are composed of 3–8 layers of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ with a layer spacing of $0.63\ \mathrm{\nm}$ . The extensive pore structure significantly shortens the charge transport distance while also buffering the volume increase produced by $\operatorname{Li}+$ embedding. While the carbon layer improves the intrinsic conductivity of the electrode material, it also provides strong structural support for the shell layer $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ to ensure structural stability. It has a specific capacity of $652\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and has been cycled for 100 cycles at a current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , demonstrating outstanding cycle stability.
Carbon is currently the most widely used anode electrode material for lithium-ion batteries. Traditional graphite materials are used as anode electrodes for commercial lithium-ion batteries to form the intercalation compound $\mathrm{LiC}_{6}$ with a theoretical specific capacity of $372{\mathrm{~mAh.g}}^{-1}$ . The use of organic ligand carbonization in MOFs to derive porous carbon materials has both porous characteristics and high conductivity and exhibits excellent lithium storage performance. Different from the effect of increasing metal sites in Section 3.2.1, the infinite mutual solubility of $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/C_{0}}}$ metal coordination in ZIF is also used [162] to prepare $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/{C O-Z I F}}}$ , control the derivatization carbonization conditions, and obtain hollow structure $\mathrm{{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{{O}_{4}}}}$ polyhedrons (Fig. 8e, f). The formation of $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/C o-}}$ ZIF particles has good uniformity and the size is about $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The porous hollow structure yields a high BET surface area of $65.58~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The nanoparticle assembly structure can expose more active sites, accelerate charge transport, and achieve high-rate performance. Porous and hollow structures can provide sufficient space to buffer volume expansion, improve structural stability, and achieve high cycle stability. Under the synergistic effect of $Z\mathrm{n_{x}}.$ ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3-\mathrm{{x}}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}$ bimetallic oxide, the difference in charge and discharge potential can effectively alleviate the structural collapse caused by stress concentration, and improve the structure and cycle stability. Even when the current density is as high as $9\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the specific capacity can still be maintained at $575\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . In addition to using the porous structure of MOFs, the structural defects caused by the high-temperature evaporation of coordination metals in MOFs can also be used to increase the specific surface area. Using Zn-PBAI as the precursor [163], it was carbonized at a high temperature of $1000~\mathrm{{^circC}}$ , and the $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous carbon material was derived in situ using the abundant N element in the organic ligand (Fig. 8g). The BET specific surface area and micropore volume of Zn-PBAI were $728~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.37~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ , respectively. The high specific surface of the porous structure comes from the MOF precursor and the microporous structure left by the evaporation of metal Zn. The presence of N enriches the active sites for lithium insertion, and the porous structure can shorten the charge transport path and improve structural stability. As anode electrode material for lithium-ion batteries, it is cycled for 200 cycles at a current density of $500\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , with a specific capacity of 1125 $\mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , exhibiting ultra-high lithium storage performance and cycle stability.
3.2.4. Regulate the host and guest of MOFs to build a functional heterogeneous structure
Combining MOFs with specific functional materials, and using the synergistic effects of each component, obtain modified composite materials that have both the specific advantages of MOFs and the advantages of functional materials, and broaden the application fields of MOFs. It is mainly classified into two categories, using MOFs as carriers for intra-particle composite or in-situ growth, or outside the particles, using electrostatic adsorption, template method, in-situ growth, etc. to composite MOFs with other materials [164–168].
Using the abundant pore structure in MOFs, composite materials, such as metal nanoparticles, quantum dots, etc. are encapsulated in the pore structure, and the channel confinement effect is used to avoid particle agglomeration and achieve uniform loading, thereby improving the utilization of active materials. C quantum dots can be added to the main liquor for preparing MOFs [166]. During the crystallization process of MOFs, C quantum dots are embedded in the pore structure in situ to form C-dots@ZIF-8. It is also possible to uniformly disperse the oxo acid salt containing Mo in the mother liquor for the preparation of MOFs [167]. During the growth of MOFs, the oxo acid salt enters the pore structure and undergoes carbonization and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ etching to obtain $\mathsf{M o C}_{\mathbf{x}}$ octahedrons (Fig. 9a).
A one-dimensional material grows along with a specific orientation, can produce directional charge transport, has a short axial electron–ion transport path, and exhibits unique electrochemical properties as an electrode material. The one-dimensional carbon nanotubes (CNT) are pretreated with anode charge modification to form electrostatic adsorption with metal ions to promote the in-situ growth of three-dimensional MOF on the surface of CNTs.
After carbonization derivatization, the MWCNTs $\mathrm{\Gamma}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}$ composite structure is obtained (Fig. 9b)[164]. Using Te nanotubes as a template [168], added to the MOFs main solution. The MOFs precursors are grown in situ on the inner and outer walls of the Te tube at the same time. After carbonizing them at a high-temperature, Te will evaporate and lose during the high temperature process, and double-walled Co-doped carbon nanotubes with the hierarchical porous structure of Co and N can be obtained, as shown in Fig. 9 (c). The electron transport of two-dimensional materials is limited to a specific crystal plane or a single plane. Compared with materials with other structures, it has more excellent conductivity and charges transport characteristics. In addition, twodimensional materials have a large specific surface area, which can expose more active sites. They can be used as a matrix to composite other materials to construct heterojunctions and use synergistic effects to accelerate electron/ion coupling. When CoAl-LDH nanosheets are used as templates [169], the similarity of metal elements in LDH and ZIF-67 is used to induce ZIF-67 to grow in situ on its surface. After carbonization, a honeycomb-like Co and N codoped porous carbon material can be obtained, as shown in Fig. 9(d). The combination of two-dimensional LDH and threedimensional MOFs can expose more effective active sites, and the prepared composite material has high conductivity while exhibiting high catalytic activity. With MXene as the matrix [170], ZIF67 is grown in situ on its surface, and after carbonization, a Co and N co-doped porous carbon/MXene composite material can be obtained, as shown in Fig. 9(e). The porous structure can provide sufficient sulfur storage space and physical barrier effect. The MXene matrix has both high conductivity and high catalytic activity, superimposed with Co/N catalytic active sites, and has a chemical anchoring effect on polysulfides [172,173]. By improving the mechanics of charge transport, the rapid conversion of soluble polysulfides is achieved and the shuttle effect is suppressed [174,175].
Using the similarity of the coordination structure between MOFs, the core–shell heterogeneous structure MOFs@MOFs composite material is constructed by heteroepitaxial method, which can play the synergistic effect of different functional MOFs materials, improve the pore structure of single MOFs, and introduce new functions at the same time. Taking ZIF series MOFs as an example, using ZIF-8 as a seed crystal [171], the ZIF-67 shell layer was epitaxially grown to build a core–shell structure ZIF-8@ZIF-67. The ZIF-8-derived porous carbon material is mainly $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon and has a relatively high specific surface area, as shown in Fig. 9 (f). The porous carbon material derived from ZIF-67 has mainly graphitized carbon under the catalysis of the Co element and has high electrical conductivity. The synergistic effect of the two ensures that the electrode material has high electrochemical performance. The high theoretical specific capacity active material is combined with a matrix material that buffers volume expansion to construct a composite electrode. Under the synergistic effect of two or more materials, a higher charge transport rate and better structural stability can be achieved. It can be summarized in two ways: composite with carbon materials and alloys. The composite of the active material and the carbon material matrix can not only improve the intrinsic conductivity but also alleviate the volume expansion and prevent the structure from collapsing. It is currently the most widely used design strategy [176,177]. The construction of alloy electrode materials mainly combines high specific volume active materials, such as Si, Sn, Sb, etc., with inactive matrix materials with high yield strength and low elastic modulus to form alloy composite materials, such as prepared $\mathsf{C u}_{6}\mathsf{S n}_{5}[178]$ , $\mathrm{FeSi_{6}}[179]$ , etc.
To achieve the goals of high energy density and high power density, there is an urgent need to develop new anode materials [180]. A series of metal compounds, including metal oxides, metal selenides [181], metal sulfides [182], and metal phosphides [183] have come into the field of researchers due to their higher theoretical capacity. Taking metal oxides as an example, the lithium storage mechanism can be roughly divided into two types: ‘‘insertionextraction reaction” and ‘‘conversion reaction” [184]. During the insertion-extraction reaction, only the lattice structure is changed, and no phase transition (such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}\mathrm{,}$ ) is involved. This process is similar to the reaction mechanism of the carbon anode [185]. For example, using titanium-based metal–organic framework material (MIL-125) as the precursor [186], anatasetype titanium dioxide was prepared, as shown in Fig. 10(a). The material has a specific surface area of up to $220\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , 500 cycles at current densities of 1C, 5C, and 10C, and reversible capacities of 166, 106, and $71\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. It is worth noting that there are not many reports on the use of metal–organic framework materials to prepare ‘‘intercalation-extraction reaction” anode materials [187,188].
Most metal oxides $\mathrm{\cdot}_{\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}}$ , $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , CuO) are ‘‘conversion reaction” type lithium-ion battery anode materials, with high theoretical capacity and chemical stability [193–195]. During the first discharge, lithium ions react with metal oxides to form $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0$ , and metal ions are reduced to metal elements; in the subsequent charging reaction process, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0$ will decompose and react with metal elements to regenerate metal oxides. The use of MOFs structure can perfectly introduce metal oxides to obtain conversion reaction type anode electrode materials. Using MIL-88 as a template [123], porous spindle-shaped ${\tt a}\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}{\tt O}_{3}$ was prepared by a two-step calcination method. In short, MIL-88 is first heattreated in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ to form a $\mathrm{FeO_{x}}$ -C composite material. Then, the resulting $\mathrm{FeO}_{\mathrm{x}^{-}}C$ composite material was sintered in air at $380^{\circ}\mathsf C$ In this way, interconnected $\mathsf{a}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{F}\mathsf{e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles (with a size less than $20\ \mathrm{\nm}$ with a spindle-like morphology were obtained. This kind of spindle-like ct- ${\mathrm{.Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{0}}_{3}$ has a reversible capacity of $911\mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after being cycled for 50 cycles at a current density of 0.2C. Under a very high current density of 10C, it can provide a reversible capacity of $424{\mathrm{~}}{\mathrm{mAh}}.{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , as shown in Fig. 4(a). The treatment process also has a great influence on the electrode materials prepared by MOFs [196,197]. Different from the abovementioned two-step calcination procedure, $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{q}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ is prepared by calcination of Fe-MOF directly in the air, and the ratio performance is not as good as that of spindle-shaped porous $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{q}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ [198]. These results indicate that the heat treatment process plays a more critical role in optimizing lithium storage performance.
It is known to all that the conductivity of metal oxides is low, and there are serious volume expansion and particle agglomeration problems during the cycle. The capacity will quickly decay after a long time cycle, and the rate performance is poor. Carbon coating and nanometerization are effective ways to solve the above-mentioned problems. Through the metal–organic framework material template method, nano-sized metal oxide and porous carbon can be easily composited. Using MOFs as the carbon source, carbon-coated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ quantum dots were prepared under an inert atmosphere [199]. Under the combined action of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and carbon, the material has a reversible capacity of $1200\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $75\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . When the heating time is extended and the heating rate is increased, the size of ZnO will increase significantly $(22\ \mathrm{nm})$ , resulting in poor lithium storage performance. This method provides a general strategy for preparing metal oxide/porous carbon nanostructures to produce highefficiency electrode materials. It is also possible to use bimetalorganic framework materials as precursors and self-sacrificial templates [189] to prepare novel porous $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}/C}$ octahedrons, which have a hollow structure (Fig. 10b). After heat treatment, about $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ ultra-small $\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{ZnFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are perfectly embedded in a conductive, porous, and interconnected carbon matrix with an internal hollow structure. As an anode electrode material for lithium-ion batteries, these formed porous $Z_{\mathrm{n}}{\mathsf{O}}/{\mathsf{Z n F e}}_{2}.$ $0_{4}/\mathsf{C}$ octahedrons exhibit excellent rate performance and excellent cycle performance. Even at a high current density of $10\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , the capacity is as high as $762~\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which is more than twice the theoretical capacity of graphite $(372\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}},$ ). In addition to metal oxides, metal sulfides, selenides, and phosphides [183] have also been reported as anode materials in lithium-ion batteries. Using Co-based metal–organic framework materials as precursors [190], a new type of porous carbon $I C0S_{2}$ composite material $(\mathsfit{C}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ ) can be prepared, as shown in Fig. 10(c). Due to the ultrasmall $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ and the porous carbon matrix rich in nitrogen, the obtained ${\mathsf C}/{\mathsf C}{\mathsf0}{\mathsf S}_{2}$ exhibits excellent lithium storage performance. The material can maintain a reversible capacity of $410\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles at a current density of $2500~\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Using ZIF-67 as a template [191], $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ nanoparticles can be encapsulated into the carbon nanotube framework and entangled with the carbon nanotubes $(\mathrm{CoSe_{2}@{N-C F/C N T s}})$ , as shown in Fig. 10(d). It is worth noting that the carbon nanotubes connected to the outer surface of the carbon skeleton are produced under the catalysis of Co nanoparticles. It not only provides a staggering conductive network to promote electrolyte penetration and charge transfer but also effectively reduces the volume expands and the structural integrity of the carbon matrix is maintained. After testing, ${\mathrm{CoSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{.}}$ - N-CF/CNTs can still provide a high reversible lithium storage capacity of $428{\mathrm{~mAh.g}}^{-1}$ after being cycled 500 times at a current density of $1000\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The theoretical specific capacity of Si is as high as $4200\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , but the volume expansion is as high as $300\%$ after the lithium is completely inserted, which can easily cause crushing and shedding of the electrode material, and the intrinsic conductivity of Si is low, which reduces the reaction kinetics. $\mathrm{{Cu-Si}}$ nanowires can be grown in situ on Cu current collectors (Fig. 10e), which effectively solves the problem of the low electrical conductivity of Si [192]. Conductive Cu nanowires ensure efficient charge transport and realize rapid coupling and separation of electrons and ions. In addition, Cu nanowires can expand in volume and provide strong structural support. The coated shell layer ${\sf A l}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ provides a stable contact interface between Si and the electrolyte, promotes the formation of a stable SEI film, and improves long-cycle stability. At a large current density of $1.4~\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the capacity can reach $1560\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles, which is four times the theoretical capacity of traditional graphite electrodes. To sum up, it can be seen that the heterostructure built by MOFs exhibits excellent electrochemical performance in lithium-ion batteries, and can effectively compensate for the defects caused by graphene as a cathode electrode.
Tian et al. [200]used $[\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{2}(\mathrm{TPT})_{2}.\mathsf{x G}]\mathsf{n}$ ( $G=$ guest molecule) as the precursor, and calcined $[\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{2}(\mathrm{TPT})_{2}.\mathbf{x}\mathbf{G}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ in air, naturally cooling to room temperature to obtain $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , the prepared $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode material maintains the $[\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{L}_{2}(\mathsf{T P T})_{2}.\mathsf{x G}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ tetrahedral structure, and the hollow structure is attributed to the $[\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{L}_{2}(\mathsf{T P T})_{2}.\mathsf{x G}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ after pyrolysis organic ligands. The electrochemical performance of the prepared $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode material was tested, and the results showed that the reversible specific capacity of $1052\ \mathrm{\mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ could still be maintained after 60 charge–discharge cycles at a current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The reversible capacity of the hollow tetrahedral $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode material is much higher than that of other reported $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode materials, as shown in Table 3.
In addition, after the second charge–discharge cycle, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode material has a Coulombic efficiency as high as nearly $98\ \%$ , showing excellent cycle reversibility. The fast charge/discharge performance is also an important factor in evaluating the excellent performance of lithium-ion batteries. Therefore, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode material was tested for 10 charge–discharge cycles at different current densities. The test results showed that the specific capacity at $50\ \mathrm{mA\cdotg^{-1}}$ current density was $1190{\mathrm{~mAh}}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , and the specific capacity at $100\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ current density was 1190 $\mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The capacity is $1125\mathrm{\mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the specific capacity is $1035~\mathrm{{mAh}}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $200\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ current density, the specific capacity is $875\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $400\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ current density, and the specific capacity is $0.8\ \mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ higher current density The lower specific capacity can reach $606\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , showing excellent rate performance. When the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode material was subjected to a series of charge–discharge cycles at different current densities, the charge–discharge cycles were performed again at a current density of $50\mathrm{mA\cdotg^{-1}}$ , and the specific capacity was $1125\mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , showing good reproducibility. Well-designed hollow or core–shell structures maximize the performance of the material, but at the same time reduce the density of the material. Therefore, it remains a great challenge to simultaneously obtain high quality, area and volume capacity, and high rate and long cycle performance for practical requirements. Due to insufficient ion diffusion and limited active sites for dense electrodes with high areal mass loading, the materials have low volumetric capacities.
When MOFs are used as cathode materials for Li-ion batteries, early work has mainly focused on a series of Fe-MOFs based on $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ as active sites for lithium storage. However, for each metal–organic framework structure, there is an upper limit on the number of intercalated Li $^+$ . When the intercalation exceeds the upper limit, further lithiation will lead to the collapse of the internal structure of the MOFs material, resulting in an unsatisfactory battery capacity of the MOFs material. Another promising material is Prussian blue and Prussian blue analogs, which are perovskite structures such as $\mathrm{K_{4}[F e(C N)_{6}]}$ [205] possessing a large number of interstitial sites. Existing studies have shown that the cycling stability of PBAs can be enhanced by suppressing the formation of vacancies, constructing a core–shell structure, or incorporating conductive additives [206,207].
## 4. Summary and outlook
Metal-organic frameworks materials are a type of periodic pore materials formed by the self-assembly of metal ions (or metal clusters) and organic ligands through coordination bonds. They have the advantages of highly controllable composition and structure, abundant pores, and large specific surface area. Through a large amount of literature research, the use of metal–organic framework materials as templates to prepare electrochemically active materials is a reliable method. This review takes the preparation of metal–organic framework derivative materials as the theme and fully demonstrates the universality and unique advantages of the metal–organic framework material template method through the reasonable construction of a variety of fine structures. The electrochemical performance of a series of metal–organic framework derivative materials in lithium-ion batteries was discussed. Because of the problems faced in practical applications, starting from the design and construction of electrochemically active materials with fine structure, by optimizing the composition and morphology structure, a multifunctional electrode material with high electrochemical activity is obtained. This has played a certain role in promoting the further development of electrochemical energy storage and conversion systems.
MOFs and their derived metal oxides still face some challenges from practical application: (1) The electrochemical stability of MOFs materials is still the primary consideration as electrode materials for lithium-ion batteries, but most MOFs are used in complex electrochemical batteries. It cannot exist stably in the environment, which is also the reason for the low rate and cycling stability of MOFs electrode materials. (2) The conductivity of MOFs and their derived metal oxides needs to be improved, which is very important for the capacity and rate performance of Li-ion batteries. Introducing carbon and metal bases into MOFs and their derived metal oxides is currently the most effective way to improve the electrical conductivity of electrode materials, and complex nanostructures can combine the advantages of various components to improve electrochemical performance. (3) At present, a certain degree of controllable synthesis of MOFs with specific structures or functions and their derived metal oxides has been achieved, and these MOFs-based electrode materials have excellent electrochemical properties, but the complex structures and electrochemical properties are not enough. The relationship between them still needs to be perfected. If the relationship between the components can be understood through sufficient experiments and theoretical analysis, it will be a better guide for the design and synthesis of functional materials with clear structural advantages. Moreover, we should also dialectically look at the shortcomings of multimorphological (core–shell, porous, nanofiber, etc.) materials as electrode materials for lithium-ion batteries, such as the complex and cumbersome synthesis process, high cost, strict processing requirements and structure. It is difficult to adjust and realize large-scale production, and the marketization of such electrode materials still faces considerable challenges. Therefore, it is necessary to adopt some relatively cheap and green raw materials and explore a simple preparation process to ensure mass production. Nowadays, many researchers try to take advantage of the unique advantages of porous structures, and by precisely controlling the synthesis conditions, a series of concentration gradients can be formed in the multi-core/multi-shell, which gradually alleviates a series of side reactions caused by the operation of lithium-ion batteries. The future development of such materials offers new directions.
When MOFs are directly used as electrode materials for lithiumion batteries, a framework with high conductivity and high stability needs to be constructed to prevent structural degradation and reduce side reactions. The electrochemical performance is improved by forming a pore structure favorable for ion and electron transport. Provide a large number and density of redox active sites to increase the battery capacity, which is conducive to the coordination of metal ions with synergistic effect. When MOFs are further processed to form MOFs derivatives, conductive substances such as carbon need to be introduced to improve the conductivity of the material, such as graphene, carbon fiber, carbon nanotubes, copper foam and nickel foam. Provide a protective layer for active electrode materials through surface modification/coating to reduce electrolytic dissolution of active electrode materials and improve cycling stability. Taking full advantage of the high porosity and high surface area of the composite material, the contact area between the electrolyte and the electrode material is increased, which is beneficial to the deintercalation of lithium ions. The directional synthesis is achieved by controlling the morphology of the composites while retaining the inherent porous structure using the template of MOFs. Combined with the current research progress of metal–organic framework derivative materials and the development trend of lithium-ion batteries, the following aspects need to be explored in the future development process:
(a) Introduce defects or heterogeneous atoms from the atomic scale. Modulate MOFs and their derivatives from a more microstructure level, combine the properties of MOFs metal sites and organic ligands, introduce heterogeneous atoms from the initial preparation process, form doping or defects, and improve intrinsic conductivity and chemical properties.   
(b) The active material derived from the metal–organic framework is composited with other conductive substrates (such as carbon cloth, etc.), and the influence of flexible electrodes and different substrates on electrochemical performance is discussed.   
(c) Further explore the general applicability of the metal–organic framework material template method. Try more possibilities in terms of composition, such as sulfides, carbides, and spinel materials. In the design of the structure, explore the method of constructing the core–shell structure and study its forming mechanism.   
(d) As the structure of MOFs has evolved from one-dimensional to three-dimensional, a powerful framework can not only effectively alleviate volume changes, but also promote the rapid diffusion or transfer of lithium ions and electrons at high current densities. Applying advanced MOFs structure to lithium-ion batteries and improving their safety, lifespan and stability are worthy of consideration.   
(e) It is not enough to determine the microscopic morphology and composition by conventional characterization methods. An in-depth analysis of the catalytic mechanism is required with the aid of DFT theoretical calculations and advanced insitu characterization methods. In the future study of electrocatalytic performance, these advanced characterization methods can lay the foundation for more detailed elucidation of the relevant catalytic mechanism.
Metal-organic frameworks and their derivatives and lithium ions are current research hotspots. How to effectively combine the two applications is our focus in the future. Although there are still some problems to be solved in current lithium-ion batteries, the unique properties of MOFs bring hope to solve this adjustment. We believe that the use of metal–organic frameworks and their derivatives to improve the lithium storage performance of lithium-ion batteries is a focus of future research.
